Cerumen Impaction
Common Infections and Afflictions
Visible obstructing wax, Conductive hearing loss, Ear fullness

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-011
Reason for Visit: Acute severe ear pain and sudden subjective deafness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute escalation of ear pain and a sudden, highly distressing loss of hearing in the right ear following a shower, accompanied by severe tinnitus and mild imbalance. The suddenness of the hearing loss, combined with acute pain and anxiety regarding permanent sensorineural damage, necessitates an urgent on-site evaluation to definitively rule out acute otitis media, tympanic membrane perforation, or sudden sensorineural hearing loss. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period poses an unacceptable risk of prolonged acute distress and potential delay in managing a more sinister otologic emergency, strictly fulfilling the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: Water got in my ear in the shower, and suddenly it went completely deaf and started throbbing intensely.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated as a vague, chronic sensation of mild ear fullness and occasional pruritus over the last three weeks. Earlier this evening, following a shower, the patient experienced a sudden, step-wise deterioration: an immediate sense of total blockage, sudden unilateral conductive hearing loss, and the onset of a loud, high-pitched tinnitus. This was rapidly followed by moderate-to-severe otalgia. Patient notes a bothersome, dry reflex cough. Denies purulent discharge, vertigo, trauma, or concurrent upper respiratory tract infection.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting high acute anxiety due to the sudden hearing loss.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Significant acute distress.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 138/85 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, cupping the right ear. Speaking loudly due to unilateral hearing deficit.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Right external auditory meatus reveals complete occlusion by a large, hard, dark-brown plug of cerumen. The cerumen is deeply impacted and visibly pressing against the deeper canal walls, which display mild reactive erythema. There is complete inability to visualize the tympanic membrane on otoscopy. Left ear canal is patent with a pearly, intact tympanic membrane. Weber test lateralizes strongly to the right ear (conductive loss). Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact. No facial droop.
Environmental Observations: Safe, brightly lit environment. Access to warm water and towels.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient attempted to use a cotton swab post-shower, likely exacerbating the deep impaction.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Cerumen Impaction - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Sudden Sensorineural Hearing Loss (SSNHL) - Definitively ruled out as the Weber test lateralizes to the affected ear (indicating a conductive deficit) and otoscopy directly visualizes an absolute physical obstruction.
Acute Otitis Media - Unlikely due to the lack of systemic viral prodrome, absence of fever, and the clear history of water exposure triggering mechanical swelling of pre-existing cerumen rather than an infectious timeline.
Foreign Body - Ruled out visually; the obstructing mass has the characteristic color, texture, and anatomical distribution of endogenous cerumen.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's acute presentation of sudden unilateral hearing loss, severe pain, and tinnitus following water exposure raised valid clinical concerns for an acute tympanic perforation or a sudden sensorineural hearing loss, both of which require immediate diagnosis to dictate time-critical management. Urgent home assessment allowed for a rapid otoscopic and neurological evaluation, definitively ruling out these emergencies and identifying acute hygroscopic expansion of impacted cerumen. By diagnosing the benign mechanical nature of the obstruction and initiating safe, targeted softening protocols, an unnecessary emergency department presentation for sudden deafness was avoided. This assessment and reassurance definitively could not wait for standard operating hours, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Docusate sodium 0.5% ear drops directly into the right ear during the consultation to initiate immediate softening. Prescribed Cerumenolytics (e.g., Docusate sodium or Olive Oil drops) to be instilled twice daily for 5 days. Syringing is strictly contraindicated at this moment due to the hardness of the impaction and complete obstruction.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the wax from re-hardening, lie on your left side for exactly 5 minutes after instilling the softening drops. This positional compliance ensures the oil pools against the deep wax plug and penetrates the dry layers, rather than immediately running out of the ear canal onto your neck.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 5-7 days for formal ear syringing or micro-suction once the cerumen is adequately softened.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the Emergency Department if experiencing sudden onset of severe room-spinning dizziness (vertigo), facial weakness, severe worsening pain, or bleeding from the ear canal.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Complete visualization of the eardrum is currently obscured; an underlying perforation cannot be 100% excluded until the wax is cleared.
Clinical Course: Hearing will remain muffled and may temporarily worsen as the drops turn the hard wax plug into a soft, expanded slurry. Resolution of deafness will only occur once the softened wax is mechanically removed by a clinician in a few days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical review if you develop a high fever, severe spreading pain behind the earbone, or if a foul-smelling liquid begins to drain from the ear.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The wax in your ear was dry. When you showered, it absorbed water, swelled up like a sponge, and physically completely blocked the canal, causing the sudden deafness and pressure pain. The drops will chemically melt this plug over a few days so it can be safely washed out.
Actionable Steps: Never insert cotton swabs, bobby pins, or fingers into the ear canal. This pushes the wax deeper, packing it against the delicate eardrum and making removal significantly more difficult and painful.

